You can get those all now at microcosmos.store.

00:00:55.152 --> 00:00:59.989
We’re going to get right to the point because
we are just too excited about today’s episode.

00:00:59.989 --> 00:01:04.980
If you’ve been following James, our master
of microscopes, on some of his other platforms,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.439
then you know what’s coming.

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:11.235
You know that James has published his first
academic paper, which was co-authored by

00:01:11.235 --> 00:01:17.371
Dr. Demetra Andreou and James’ mentor, Professor
Genoveva F. Esteban.

00:01:17.371 --> 00:01:21.200
If you want to find it, just look for the
article titled “The Extraordinarily Rare

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.430
Ciliate Legendrea loyezae” in the journal
Protist.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:28.009
We’ll leave a link for it down below.

00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:32.439
The paper is, as you might be able to tell
from the title, about this extraordinarily

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:36.993
rare ciliate that you see on the screen 
now called Legendrea loyezae.

00:01:36.993 --> 00:01:38.979
Just how rare is this ciliate?

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:44.520
It’s so rare that before James stumbled
on some of his own, it had only been reported

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.471
by four other people, ever.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.250
There are a vast number of microbe species
in the world.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:59.585
So many, in fact, that it seems like a miracle
that we are able to find the same species twice.

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:04.950
Some of these species are incredibly prolific,
reproducing at rates that make them impossible

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.930
to ignore under the microscope.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:14.349
But then there are the microbes who turn up
once in a blue moon, like a mythical creature

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:22.370
of the microcosmos whose life seems to exist
only in a few lines of text and the rare illustrations

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.319
that preserve them.

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:30.620
The first record of Legendrea loyezae’s
existence came in 1908, from a French scientist

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.350
named Emmanuel Fauré-Fremiet.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:38.940
He had been gathering samples from a pond
near Paris, studying the mud for what he described

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.950
as “infusoria”, a name once used to describe
microbes.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:50.110
And among the organisms he found were just
a few of these oval-ish creatures, with one

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:57.600
elongated macronucleus and roughly 20 tentacles
of varying length that branch out from their bodies.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.866
He named the creature Legendrea loyezae, though
he never explained why.

00:03:02.866 --> 00:03:08.770
Only six years later, a Swiss biologist named
Eugene Penard reported finding Legendrea loyezae

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:12.690
in his own samples, this time from Geneva.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.570
But he didn’t just stop with Legendrea loyezae.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:21.490
Penard found six other species that he believed
should belong to the genus, including this

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:25.182
one, which he named Legendrea bellerophon.

00:03:25.182 --> 00:03:30.840
There were a few characteristics that made
Penard consider this a new, separate species.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:36.500
But the most important factor were Legendrea
bellerophon’s tentacles, which Penard described

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:42.230
as really more like “sessile pimples”
that could extend and retract.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:48.000
In comparison, Legendrea loyezae were not capable
of the controlled extension

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.484
that Legendrea bellerophon are.

00:03:50.484 --> 00:03:56.110
Instead, their tentacles trail behind as they
swim through the water.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:01.860
Alfred Kahl, the prolific documenter of ciliate
life, reported his description of Legendrea

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.300
loyezae in 1930.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:12.280
But after that, they seemed to go unnoticed
in the microcosmos until 2014, when a microscopist

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:17.270
named Martin Kreutz found them lurking in
German ponds.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:22.340
And across all of these descriptions were
slight contradictions, differences in how

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:29.600
they perceived the cell’s morphology that
were difficult to resolve with so few observations.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.500
But even in those large gaps of time where
scientists weren’t finding Legendrea loyezae,

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.250
they were not forgotten.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:39.389
In 1967, the scientist A.W.

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:45.248
Jankowski decided to take an ax to the Legendrea
genus, splitting it into three.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.370
The distinction was based on the placement
of the organism’s tentacles.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:58.580
Legendrea like Legendrea loyezae who had tentacles
at the end of the cell would remain Legendrea.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:04.710
Those who had tentacles only halfway in a
row around the cell would become Lacerus.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:09.350
And those who had extendable tentacles in
a row that goes around the cell would be called

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.310
Thysanomorpha.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:15.921
And that’s how Legendrea bellerophon became
Thysanomorpha bellerophon.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:24.220
It’s not clear why Jankowski made this decision
to split Legendrea into three genera, nor

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:29.270
is it clear why it was accepted in the official
taxonomic codes.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:34.980
Jankowski didn’t seem to have made any observations
of his own to decide these differences, instead

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:41.710
using the discrepancies in the rare, existing
observations to make vast changes in taxonomy,

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:44.821
without accounting for other possibilities.

00:05:44.821 --> 00:05:51.203
But that brings us to 2021 and the discovery
of another possibility.

00:05:51.203 --> 00:05:57.700
More specifically, it brings us to James,
equipped with a plastic bottle, a microscope,

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:00.733
and an endless devotion to his craft.

00:06:05.462 --> 00:06:11.150
Visiting two lakes around Warsaw, James carefully
gathered samples from the bottom of the lakes

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:16.780
and stored them in the dark, with lids shut
tight to minimize the amount of oxygen coming

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:22.599
in so that the anaerobic organisms that live
in these sediments could thrive.

00:06:22.599 --> 00:06:29.090
And when he went to see what was hiding within
his samples, he found four Legendrea loyezae.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:33.810
He immediately recognized it from the pages
of his beloved ciliate reference books and

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:37.560
was thrilled to tell his mentor, Professor
Esteban.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.620
But the most surprising thing would come later,
in a microscope slide James sealed off with

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:48.000
petroleum jelly so that he could watch this
Legendrea loyezae for longer.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.440
He’d been watching it swim around, its tentacles
trailing behind.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:57.729
And he kept the slide in a humidity chamber and
returned to it over several days, watching

00:06:57.729 --> 00:07:00.672
as it settled down among some debris.

00:07:00.672 --> 00:07:07.320
It was clearly alive because there was movement
in its cytoplasm, but it just didn’t move,

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:12.450
even when James lightly tapped the slide or
shined strong light on it.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:15.650
But after four days, something strange happened.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:20.919
This green euglenid wandered close to the
Legendrea, too close, apparently, for the

00:07:20.919 --> 00:07:26.530
Legendrea’s comfort, causing it to contract
its tentacles.

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:30.699
We still don’t know what exactly set the
Legendrea into action, but the most likely

00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:35.353
explanation is that the euglenid was releasing
oxygen into the water,

00:07:35.353 --> 00:07:38.660
discomforting the anaerobic Legendrea.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:44.340
What caught James and Professor Esteban’s
notice was that Legendrea loyezae seemed to

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:50.070
not only control the length of its tentacles,
but also the location.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:56.000
And as they extended and contracted their
tentacles, the organism’s shape changed.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.729
When extended, the tentacles appeared towards
the side of the swollen organism.

00:08:01.729 --> 00:08:07.419
When contracted, the tentacles sprouted out
from the end of a heart-shaped cell.

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:13.260
No one had ever observed this tentacle extension
in Legendrea loyezae before.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:19.240
And more importantly, this movement seems
to explain the differences between the descriptions

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:23.090
of Legendrea loyezae made by earlier scientists.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:31.280
The different shapes they observed were likely
Legendrea loyezae in different states of tentacle contraction.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.950
And this is really meaningful because it muddles the
reasoning behind Jankowski’s division of

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:42.890
the Legendrea, which was derived in part on
a static understanding of the tentacles.

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:49.250
And as James argues in the paper, it means
that Thysanomorpha bellerophon should become

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:52.330
Legendrea bellerophon once more.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:58.790
These observations that James made with his
microscope revealed a lot about a rare species.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:04.750
But there are depths that the microscope cannot
reach, information hidden in places we need

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:06.619
other techniques to reach.

00:09:06.619 --> 00:09:09.890
We’ll talk about that in our next episode.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:10.960
And who knows.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:16.070
With more observation and more data, perhaps
there will be more species reverted back to

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:17.594
the Legendrea genus.

00:09:17.594 --> 00:09:20.730
Because that’s the point of science.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:25.820
There is no end to what we know and what we
will learn.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:30.029
There is simply a patient dedication to the
work.

00:09:30.029 --> 00:09:33.440
To visiting the same pond week after week.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.970
To watching the same sample day after day.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:44.090
To digging through old books and forgotten
texts until they are embedded in memory.

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:48.510
We do love quoting a microscopist here on
Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:54.267
So perhaps we should pay tribute to our own
master of microscopes and the ending of his paper.

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:59.779
We hope that the study here presented inspires
the readership to spend more time looking

00:09:59.779 --> 00:10:06.839
through a microscope and reminding them that
science is done to subside the mind’s curiosity,

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:11.770
not incite discord in one’s rather short
lifespan.

